1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:15,670 --> 00:00:19,418
Today, I'm going to talk
about the fragment class.

3
00:00:19,418 --> 00:00:25,176
Fragments were added to Android in version
3.0 to better support user interfaces for

4
00:00:25,176 --> 00:00:28,620
de, devices with large screens,
such as tablets.

5
00:00:29,710 --> 00:00:31,310
Now, over the last few years,

6
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:34,230
the popularity of tablets
has grown incredibly.

7
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:39,540
And as I'm sure you know, tablets have
larger displays than phones typically do.

8
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:44,520
So, because of these larger screens,
some of the design heuristics that made

9
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:48,850
sense for a smaller phone display,
no longer work.

10
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:54,060
In particular, we can comfortably do
much more on a large tablet display,

11
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:56,260
than we could do on a small phone display.

12
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:59,700
Now, to put this in concrete terms,

13
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:04,345
let's take a look at a simple
application called QuoteViewer.

14
00:01:04,345 --> 00:01:08,620
QuoteViewer is an application
comprised of two activities.

15
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:13,100
The first activity shows the titles
of several Shakespeare plays and

16
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:16,590
allows the user to select
one title at a time.

17
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:21,820
And when the user selects that title, a
second activity is started which displays

18
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:28,670
one quote from that play,
let's take a look.

19
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:34,330
Now I'll launch the QuoteViewer
application, the first activity starts up.

20
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:39,930
And shows us the titles of three
Shakespeare plays, Hamlet, King Lear, and

21
00:01:39,930 --> 00:01:40,650
Julius Ceasar.

22
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:42,920
Let me click on Hamlet.

23
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:48,040
This starts the second
activity which shows me

24
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:51,180
Horatio's quote as Hamlet is dying.

25
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:53,190
Now cracks a noble heart.

26
00:01:53,190 --> 00:01:56,610
Good night sweet prince, and
flights of angels sing thee to thy rest.

27
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:00,730
Now I'll hit the Back button
to return to the titles.

28
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:03,758
And next, I'll click on King Lear.

29
00:02:03,758 --> 00:02:09,802
And again, we get a quote, this time,
one from the blinded Earl of Gloucester

30
00:02:09,802 --> 00:02:15,230
who says, as flies to wanton boys, are we
to the gods, they kill us for their sport.

31
00:02:16,470 --> 00:02:20,390
And once again, I hit the Back
button to return to the titles.

32
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:23,320
And this time I click on Julius Ceasar.

33
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:27,770
And again, the second activity
starts up and we see a quote.

34
00:02:27,770 --> 00:02:32,630
This one, where Brutus urges
Cassius to go on the attack.

35
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:37,580
He says, there is a tide in the affairs
of men, which taken at the flood,

36
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,620
leads on to fortune.

37
00:02:39,620 --> 00:02:44,980
Omitted, all the voyage of their life
is bound in shallows and in miseries.

38
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:51,030
Now this is a fine interface for a phone.

39
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:54,790
In fact, it would be hard to do much
else and still have a readable and

40
00:02:54,790 --> 00:02:56,860
easy to use interface.

41
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:59,780
On a tablet, however,
this same layout is pretty lame.

42
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:01,632
Now here, let me show you.

43
00:03:04,647 --> 00:03:06,630
Here I'm showing an emulated tablet.

44
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,100
I'll click on the Launcher icon.

45
00:03:10,890 --> 00:03:14,740
And now I'll start the QuoteViewer
application we just saw, on the phone.

46
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:19,740
And the first thing you notice
is all that unused white space.

47
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:24,310
The titles take up just a little space
on the left side of the display, and

48
00:03:24,310 --> 00:03:26,590
the rest is pretty much wasted.

49
00:03:26,590 --> 00:03:27,700
Now let me click on Hamlet.

50
00:03:29,150 --> 00:03:34,200
And here you see that the quote stretches
all the way across the ten inch tablet but

51
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,070
uses little of the vertical space.

52
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:40,870
Now I'll hit the Back button
to go back to the titles.

53
00:03:40,870 --> 00:03:43,540
And I can do the same thing for
the King Lear quote.

54
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:52,090
And the same thing for
the Julius Caesar quote.

55
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:55,630
And besides not being,

56
00:03:55,630 --> 00:04:00,520
not using the available space too well,
this layout imposes a slow back and

57
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:04,210
forth navigation style where the user
has to keep moving their hand from

58
00:04:04,210 --> 00:04:09,290
the title then down to the back button,
then back to the title and so on.

59
00:04:09,290 --> 00:04:14,280
So, let's see a better layout that
uses both or shows both the titles and

60
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:15,930
the quotes at the same time.

61
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:19,995
I'll click on the Launcher icon and

62
00:04:19,995 --> 00:04:24,510
then start up the fragment
static layout application.

63
00:04:24,510 --> 00:04:28,370
And this initially brings up a user
interface that looks exactly like what you

64
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:31,420
saw with the QuoteViewer application.

65
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:34,190
However, when I click on the title,

66
00:04:34,190 --> 00:04:39,700
Hamlet, you see that the user interface
gets essentially divided into two parts.

67
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,820
The titles stay where they
were on the left, but

68
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:47,860
the quote is now shown simultaneously
on the right side of the screen.

69
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:50,150
And now if I want to
see a different quote,

70
00:04:50,150 --> 00:04:52,739
I simply select a new
play title on the left.

71
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:55,560
Here I'll select King Lear.

72
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:57,800
And again, the quote pops up on the right.

73
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:01,270
And the same thing when
I select Julius Caesar.

74
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:05,529
Now overall,
this layout works much better on

75
00:05:05,529 --> 00:05:08,570
a tablet than QuoteViewer's layout does.

76
00:05:09,810 --> 00:05:12,250
In particular, it avoids wasted space, and

77
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:15,130
it makes navigation a little bit
easier because I don't have to

78
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:18,980
keep moving my hands down to the bottom
of the display to hit the Back button.

79
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:20,972
And then up to the titles, and so on.

80
00:05:20,972 --> 00:05:29,000
The QuoteViewer application
was made up of two activities.

81
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,500
The fragment static layout
application that we just saw,

82
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:36,540
however, is made up of a single activity.

83
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:41,100
That single activity,
however, hosts two fragments.

84
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:46,130
Fragments represent the portion
of an activity's user interface.

85
00:05:46,130 --> 00:05:48,500
The fragment static layout application,
for

86
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:52,120
example, has two such interface portions.

87
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:57,830
One for the titles goes on the left and
one for the quotes that goes on the right.

88
00:05:57,830 --> 00:06:03,070
And each of these portions of the user
interface is implemented as a fragment.

89
00:06:04,590 --> 00:06:07,700
Fragments are hosted by activities.

90
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:11,750
One activity can host zero or
more fragments.

91
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:15,650
And one fragment can be used
across zero or more activities.

92
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:21,530
Now as I said,
fragments are hosted by activities.

93
00:06:21,530 --> 00:06:25,190
So fragments have to be
loaded into the activity and

94
00:06:25,190 --> 00:06:31,520
then displayed, removed, and so
on as the activity changes its state.

95
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:35,990
And as a result the lifecycle
of a fragment is tied to and

96
00:06:35,990 --> 00:06:41,300
coordinated with the lifecycle of
the activity that's hosting it.

97
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:47,900
At the same time however, fragments have
some of their own life cycle call backs.

98
00:06:50,270 --> 00:06:53,290
For now,
let's talk about the fragment life cycle,

99
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:57,830
assuming that the fragment is statically
bound to its hosting activity.

100
00:06:57,830 --> 00:07:00,020
We'll talk about dynamically adding and

101
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:03,300
removing fragments from a hosting
activity a little bit later.

102
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:09,460
So, a fragment can be in a running or
resumed state.

103
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:14,050
And when it's in this state, the fragment
is visible in the running activity.

104
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:18,690
A fragment can also be
in a paused state and

105
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:22,633
fragments will be paused when their
hosting activity is visible but

106
00:07:22,633 --> 00:07:26,060
some other activity is in
the foreground and has focus.

107
00:07:28,150 --> 00:07:31,000
A fragment can also be in a stopped state.

108
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,050
In this state,
the fragment is not visible.

109
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:37,940
Let's talk now about the life
cycle callback methods

110
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:39,090
that a fragment can receive.

111
00:07:40,210 --> 00:07:44,100
And we'll talk about when those
methods might be called relative to

112
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:48,370
the life cycle states of the activity
that hosts the fragment.

113
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:54,130
When a fragment's hosting
activity is being created,

114
00:07:54,130 --> 00:07:57,880
the fragment may receive several
life cycle method calls.

115
00:07:58,890 --> 00:08:03,460
First, when a fragment is
attached to its hosting activity,

116
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:06,489
the fragment receives a call to onAttach.

117
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:12,000
Next, the fragment receives
a call to its onCreate method.

118
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:17,351
And note here that I'm referring to
the fragment.onCreate method, not

119
00:08:17,351 --> 00:08:22,402
the activity.onCreate method that we've
talked so much about in previous lessons.

120
00:08:24,870 --> 00:08:28,025
And as with the actitity.onCreate method,

121
00:08:28,025 --> 00:08:33,421
you typically initialize the fragment
in the fragment's onCreate method,

122
00:08:33,421 --> 00:08:37,986
one difference between the two
method however, is that unlike in

123
00:08:37,986 --> 00:08:43,840
activity.onCreate you don't set up
the user interface in fragment.onCreate.

124
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:46,030
That actually happens in the next method.

125
00:08:48,410 --> 00:08:54,810
Now, after onCreate, Android calls
the fragments onCreateView method.

126
00:08:54,810 --> 00:09:01,960
And this method sets up and returns a view
containing the fragment's user interface.

127
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:05,330
And this view is then given
to the hosting activity so

128
00:09:05,330 --> 00:09:10,520
it can install that view in
the activity's view hierarchy.

129
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:14,550
And finally, after the hosting
activity has been created and

130
00:09:14,550 --> 00:09:17,515
the fragment's user interface
has been installed,

131
00:09:17,515 --> 00:09:22,357
then the fragment receives a call
to it's onActivityCreated method.

132
00:09:24,070 --> 00:09:28,221
Now, while the fragment is
attached to the hosting activity,

133
00:09:28,221 --> 00:09:32,720
It's life'cycle is dependent
on it's hosts life cycle.

134
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:37,270
For example, if the hosting activity
is about to become visible,

135
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:39,420
it will receive a call
from its onStart method.

136
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:45,200
The fragment therefore will also
receive a call to its onStart method.

137
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:50,180
When the hosting activity is about
to become visible and ready for

138
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:54,410
user interaction Android calls
the fragments onResume method.

139
00:09:56,140 --> 00:09:59,730
And when the hosting activity is visible
but another activity is about to

140
00:09:59,730 --> 00:10:04,260
come into the foreground, Android
calls the fragment's onPause method.

141
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:07,260
And when the hosting activity is no

142
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:11,140
longer visible the fragment
receives a call to onStop.

143
00:10:13,050 --> 00:10:17,450
Now, when he hosting activity is about
to be destroyed any fragments that

144
00:10:17,450 --> 00:10:21,020
it's hosting also have to be shut down.

145
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:23,210
And, this happens in several steps.

146
00:10:23,210 --> 00:10:26,530
First, when the fragments
user interface view,

147
00:10:26,530 --> 00:10:31,570
the one that was previously created by the
call to onCreateView, when that view has

148
00:10:31,570 --> 00:10:38,830
been detached from the activity, the
fragment receives a call to onDestroyView.

149
00:10:38,830 --> 00:10:42,240
And here you can do things like clean
up resources associated with the view.

150
00:10:44,330 --> 00:10:47,370
Next, when the fragment
is no longer in use,

151
00:10:47,370 --> 00:10:50,250
it receives a call to
it's onDestroy method.

152
00:10:50,250 --> 00:10:53,750
And here you can do things like
release fragment resources.

153
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:59,410
Next, when the fragment is no longer
attached to its hosting activity,

154
00:10:59,410 --> 00:11:01,920
it will receive a call to onDetach.

155
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:06,362
And here you might do things like
null out references to the fragment's

156
00:11:06,362 --> 00:11:07,303
hosting activity.

